        "context_document_id": "6851dca7-50b8-4ceb-a02c-8958d360af6a",
        "contextDocumentText": "\n# Formstack Forms (Live Forms) Authentication Troubleshooting Queries\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive collection of Sumo Logic queries specifically designed for troubleshooting Formstack Forms (Live Forms) authentication issues. These queries enable support teams and developers to quickly diagnose SSO/SAML authentication problems, track user authentication journeys, and identify configuration issues across the platform. By leveraging structured logging data from the authentication flow, these queries help isolate root causes of authentication failures, monitor SSO adoption patterns, and ensure proper SSO/SAML configuration across different identity providers (IdPs) such as OneLogin, Okta, Azure/Entra ID, and others. The queries are organized to cover the complete authentication lifecycle from initial configuration through successful user authentication, making them invaluable for both proactive monitoring and reactive troubleshooting of authentication-related issues.\n\n## Queries\n\n#### Auth provider mismatch errors by account and form\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"auth_provider_saml\" \"SAML Form Authentication Auth Provider Mismatch\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, formId, redirectUrlPath | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query identifies instances where users attempted to complete SSO/SAML authentication but the auth provider ID in their session didn't match the provider configured on the target form. This typically occurs when a user authenticates through one IdP (Identity Provider) but tries to access a form requiring a different IdP. The aggregation by form and account helps identify which forms are experiencing configuration issues or where users might be confused about which IdP to use.\n\n\n#### Incomplete manual SSO/SAML configuration attempts\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"Updating SAML settings manually for Form Authentication provider\" | json auto | where entityIdSet=false or ssoUrlSet=false or certProvided=false | fields _message, accountId, authProviderId, userId, entityIdSet, ssoUrlSet, certProvided, _raw\n```\n\nThis query tracks when administrators manually update SSO/SAML configuration settings through the admin interface but leave required fields empty. It filters for cases where the entity ID, SSO URL, or x509 certificate was not provided, which indicates incomplete configuration. This is useful for identifying accounts that may need assistance with SSO/SAML setup or are experiencing configuration difficulties, as manual entry is more error-prone than automated metadata import.\n\n\n#### SSO/SAML metadata imports via URL\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"Importing SAML metadata via URL\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, userId, metadataUrl | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query shows when users import SSO/SAML Identity Provider (IdP) metadata by providing a metadata URL rather than manually entering configuration details. This automated import method is preferred as it reduces configuration errors and automatically populates all required SSO/SAML settings (entity ID, SSO URL, and certificate). The aggregation helps identify which metadata URLs are being used most frequently and can help spot patterns in IdP usage across accounts.\n\n\n#### SSO/SAML metadata imports via file upload\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"Importing SAML metadata via file upload\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, userId, formDataKey | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query tracks SSO/SAML IdP metadata imports done via file upload instead of URL or manual entry. Like URL imports, this method automates configuration by parsing the uploaded XML metadata file to extract all required SSO/SAML settings. This approach is common when the IdP doesn't provide a publicly accessible metadata URL or when users prefer to download and upload the metadata file directly from their IdP's admin console.\n \n\n#### X509 certificate parsing failures\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"auth_provider_saml\" \"Unable to parse x509 certificate\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, userId | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query identifies failures in parsing or validating x509 certificates provided for SSO/SAML authentication. The system attempts to validate certificates using PHP's openssl functions and supports both PEM and DER/CER formats with automatic conversion. This error typically indicates the certificate file is corrupted, in an unsupported format, or not a valid x509 certificate. Aggregating by account and user helps identify who may need assistance with obtaining or providing a properly formatted certificate from their IdP.\n\n\n#### Auth provider metadata processing errors\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"auth_provider_saml\" \"Error processing authProvider\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, mysqlError | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query captures errors that occur when attempting to refresh SSO/SAML metadata from the original metadata URL during the authentication flow. Despite these errors, the system continues with authentication using existing cached metadata to provide resilience when the IdP's metadata endpoint is temporarily unavailable. This query helps identify auth providers that may have invalid metadata URLs, network connectivity issues, or IdP endpoints that are frequently down, which could eventually cause authentication failures if the cached metadata expires.\n\n\n\n\n#### New auth provider creation events\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"Created new Form Authentication AuthProvider\" or \"AuthProvider created\" | json auto | count by accountId, userId, authProviderId, name | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query tracks the creation of new Form Authentication providers across both the controller and service layers. The dual logging approach (captured at both layers) ensures creation events are recorded even if one logging call fails or the service is called from different entry points. This helps track SSO adoption patterns, identify which users are setting up authentication providers, and monitor the growth of Form Authentication usage across the platform.\n\n\n\n\n\n#### Successful SSO journey by formId (who/when/what)\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n(\"form_render\" or \"form_view\" or \"sso_authentication\" or \"saml_authentication\" or \"auth_successful\")\n| json auto\n| where formId=_FORM_ID_\n| sort by _messageTime asc\n| fields _messageTime, message, formId, accountId, authProviderId, userId, redirectUrlPath\n```\n\nThis query traces the complete user authentication journey for a specific form, from initial form access through successful SSO completion. It uses multiple event types that represent different stages: form_render/form_view for initial form loading, sso_authentication/saml_authentication for the authentication flow, and auth_successful for completion. Sorting chronologically and including key identifiers helps reconstruct exactly what happened during a user's SSO session, making it invaluable for debugging authentication issues or understanding the user experience.\n\n\n\n\n#### Confirm ACS/finalize success events for the form (tie to user/authProvider)\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n(\"sso_auth\" or \"saml_authentication\" or \"auth_successful\")\n| json auto\n| where formId=_FORM_ID_\n| sort by _messageTime asc\n| fields _messageTime, message, formId, accountId, authProviderId, userId, ip\n```\n\nThis query focuses specifically on the Assertion Consumer Service (ACS) endpoint and authentication finalization events for a form. It narrows the scope to just the SSO/SAML authentication and success events, filtering out form rendering events. This is useful for confirming that users are successfully completing the SSO/SAML response processing and being authenticated, and for tying specific authentication events to users, auth providers, and IP addresses for security auditing purposes.\n\n\n#### Redirect back fails due to provider mismatch (find by formId)\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"auth_provider_saml\" \"SAML Form Authentication Auth Provider Mismatch\"\n| json auto\n| where formId=_FORM_ID_\n| sort by _messageTime asc\n```\n\nThis query is a form-specific version of the auth provider mismatch query, useful for debugging why users accessing a particular form are experiencing authentication failures. When a user authenticates through one IdP but the form expects a different IdP, this mismatch is logged with the specific form ID. Sorting chronologically helps identify patterns in when these failures occur and whether they correlate with configuration changes or user behavior.\n\n\n#### Successful SSO/SAML authentication events by auth provider\n```sumologic\n \"SAML Authentication Succeeded\" samlUserData\n| json field=_raw \"context.authProviderId\" as authProviderId\n| where authProviderId = _AUTH_PROVIDER_ID_\n```\n\nThis query retrieves all successful SSO/SAML authentication events for a specific auth provider. Each success event includes rich context about the authentication including which certificate was used, all SSO/SAML claim keys received, and (for non-HIPAA accounts) the full SSO/SAML response and user data. This is the primary success indicator for SSO/SAML authentication journeys and is useful for confirming an auth provider is working correctly, auditing successful logins, and examining what user data is being received from the IdP.\n\n\nFull Sumo Message (minus `response:_XML_`)\nHere's your JSON pretty-printed for readability:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"message\": \"SSO/SAML Authentication Succeeded\",\n  \"context\": {\n    \"authProviderId\": \"_AUTH_PROVIDER_ID_\",\n    \"accountId\": \"_ACCOUNT_ID_\",\n    \"certificateUsed\": 0,\n    \"samlDataClaimKeys\": [\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/tenantid\",\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/objectidentifier\",\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/displayname\",\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/identityprovider\",\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/claims/authnmethodsreferences\",\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/givenname\",\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/surname\",\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/emailaddress\",\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/name\",\n      \"email\"\n    ],\n    \"response\": \"<?xml version=\\\"1.0\\\"?>\\n<samlp:Response ... </samlp:Response>\\n\",\n    \"samlUserData\": {\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/tenantid\": [\n        \"_TENANT_ID_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/objectidentifier\": [\n        \"_OBJECT_IDENTIFIER_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/displayname\": [\n        \"_DISPLAY_NAME_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/identityprovider\": [\n        \"_IDENTITY_PROVIDER_URL_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/claims/authnmethodsreferences\": [\n        \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/ws/2008/06/identity/authenticationmethod/password\",\n        \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/claims/multipleauthn\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/givenname\": [\n        \"_FIRST_NAME_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/surname\": [\n        \"_LAST_NAME_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/emailaddress\": [\n        \"_EMAIL_ADDRESS_\"\n      ],\n      \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/name\": [\n        \"_USERNAME_\"\n      ],\n      \"email\": \"_EMAIL_\"\n    },\n    \"event\": \"sso_saml_authentication\",\n    \"sessionId\": \"_SESSION_ID_\",\n    \"errorId\": \"_ERROR_ID_\"\n  },\n  \"level\": 200,\n  \"level_name\": \"INFO\",\n  \"channel\": \"formstackAppLog\",\n  \"datetime\": \"2025-09-08T13:50:58.117495-04:00\"\n}\n```\n\n",
        "contextDocumentTextNormalized": "Formstack Forms (Live Forms) Authentication Troubleshooting Queries\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive collection of Sumo Logic queries specifically designed for troubleshooting Formstack Forms (Live Forms) authentication issues. These queries enable support teams and developers to quickly diagnose SSO/SAML authentication problems, track user authentication journeys, and identify configuration issues across the platform. By leveraging structured logging data from the authentication flow, these queries help isolate root causes of authentication failures, monitor SSO adoption patterns, and ensure proper SSO/SAML configuration across different Identity Providers (IdPs) such as OneLogin, Okta, Azure/Entra ID, and others. The queries are organized to cover the complete authentication lifecycle from initial configuration through successful user authentication, making them invaluable for both proactive monitoring and reactive troubleshooting of authentication-related issues.\n\nQueries\n\nAuth provider mismatch errors by account and form\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"auth_provider_saml\" \"SAML Form Authentication Auth Provider Mismatch\" | json auto | count by accountId, authProviderId, formId, redirectUrlPath | sort by _count desc\n```\n\nThis query identifies instances where users attempted to complete SSO/SAML authentication but the auth provider ID in their session didn't match the provider configured on the target form. This typically occurs when a user authenticates through one IdP (Identity Provider) but tries to access a form requiring a different IdP. The aggregation by form and account helps identify which forms are experiencing configuration issues or where users might be confused about which IdP to use.\n\nIncomplete manual SSO/SAML configuration attempts\n```sumologic\n_sourceCategory=formstack/prod/*\n\"Updating SAML settings manually for Form Authentication provider\" | json auto | where 